              "text": "How long sliced American cheese lasts after buying depends entirely on the **type of American cheese** you bought (individually wrapped pre-packaged slices vs. freshly sliced cheese from the deli counter) and how it is stored. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Pre-Packaged, Individually Wrapped Slices (e.g., Kraft Singles)\nBecause these processed cheese slices contain emulsifiers, preservatives, and are individually sealed, they have a remarkably long shelf life.\n* **Unopened:** They can easily last **1 to 2 months** *past* the printed \"best-by\" or \"sell-by\" date on the package, provided they have been kept continuously refrigerated. \n* **Opened (outer package open, individual slices still wrapped):** They will remain safe and of high quality in the fridge for **1 to 2 months**. Make sure to keep the outer bag sealed tight or place them in a resealable zip-top bag to keep them from drying out.\n\n### 2. Freshly Sliced Deli American Cheese (e.g., Land O'Lakes sliced at the counter)\nFreshly sliced deli cheese does not contain the same heavy preservatives or individual plastic wrapping as singles. It is much more susceptible to bacteria and mold.\n* **Unopened (in original deli bag):** It can last for **2 to 3 weeks** in the refrigerator. \n* **Opened:** Once you open the deli paper/bag, it is highly recommended to consume it within **5 to 7 days** for the best quality and safety. \n\n---\n\n### Can You Freeze Sliced American Cheese?\n**Yes, but with a caveat.** You can freeze sliced American cheese for up to **6 to 8 months**. To do so, wrap the cheese tightly in aluminum foil, plastic freezer wrap, or place it in a heavy-duty freezer bag. \n* *Note:* Freezing changes the physical structure of cheese. When thawed, the slices may become dry, crumbly, or stick together. Frozen American cheese is best reserved for melting (like in grilled cheese, burgers, or sauces) rather than eating cold.\n\n---\n\n### Signs of Spoilage (How to Tell if It's Bad)\nUnlike hard block cheeses (where you can safely cut mold off the edges), **sliced cheese must be thrown away entirely if it goes bad**. Look out for:\n* **Mold:** Any fuzzy spots of white, green, blue, or grey.\n* **Texture changes:** Slices that have become excessively slimy, watery, or conversely, extremely hard, dry, and cracked around the edges.\n* **Smell:** A sour, yeasty, or ammonia-like odor.\n* **Taste:** If you take a bite and it tastes sour or off, spit it out and discard the package.",
